</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Training:</span>
Our implementation of CQ2D is based on mmdetection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>, and the implementation of CQ3D extends the original code from MonoDETR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>. Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>, we increase the number of queries to 1000.
If not stated otherwise, we set the loss weight <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> to 1 for the 2D model and to 0.5 for the 3D model.
Both models use <span id="S4.SS1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text"><math id="S4.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{\text{bins}}=121" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mtext>bins</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>121</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{\text{bins}}=121</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span id="S4.SS1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text"><math id="S4.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{x/y}=\frac{1}{3}w_{\text{bbox}}/h_{\text{bbox}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mtext>bbox</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mtext>bbox</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{x/y}=\frac{1}{3}w_{\text{bbox}}/h_{\text{bbox}}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.
The density value range for the embedding is set to <span id="S4.SS1.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text"><math id="S4.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\rho_{\text{min}}=0,\rho_{\text{max}}=3]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mtext>min</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mtext>max</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\rho_{\text{min}}=0,\rho_{\text{max}}=3]</annotation></semantics></math></span>.
The lower bound <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{\text{min}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mtext>min</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{\text{min}}</annotation></semantics></math> is always set to zero to cover empty regions, while the upper bound is set based on the dataset characteristics.
For STCrowd, we found an average number of <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.02" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.02</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.02</annotation></semantics></math> overlapping triplets per image, justifying our upper density limit.
We define overlapping triplets by a shared <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{\acs{IoU}}\geq 0.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_missing"><a href="#id1"><abbr href="#id1" title="CrowdQuery: Density-Guided Query Module for Enhanced 2D and 3D Detection in Crowded Scenes" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">IoU</abbr></a></mtext><mo>≥</mo><mn>0.3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{\acs{IoU}}\geq 0.3</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In our experiments, both models were trained on a single NVIDIA A100 GPU with a batch size of 8.
For the 2D training, we employed a learning rate of <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math> and trained for 40 epochs.
The learning rate was reduced by a factor of 2 at epochs 25 and 35.
Regarding the 3D training, we adhered to the settings of MonoDETR.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">IV-B</span> </span><span id="S4.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Results</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We report 2D detection results on the STCrowd dataset in Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="TABLE I ‣ IV-A Experimental Setup ‣ IV EXPERIMENTS ‣ CrowdQuery: Density-Guided Query Module for Enhanced 2D and 3D Detection in Crowded Scenes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">I</span></a>.
DDAD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> is excluded from the baselines, as we were unable to reproduce meaningful detection performance with our local training.
Compared to all other detection methods, our proposed <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CQ2D</span> achieves the best <a href="#id4"><abbr href="#id4" title="CrowdQuery: Density-Guided Query Module for Enhanced 2D and 3D Detection in Crowded Scenes" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">AP</abbr></a> with <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="91.4\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>91.4</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">91.4\%</annotation></semantics></math>, outperforming the current best approach GigaHumanDet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>, by <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.7</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.7</annotation></semantics></math> points.
Extending the baseline Deformable DETR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> model by our proposed <a href="#id7"><abbr href="#id7" title="CrowdQuery: Density-Guided Query Module for Enhanced 2D and 3D Detection in Crowded Scenes" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">CQ</abbr></a> module improves the <a href="#id4"><abbr href="#id4" title="CrowdQuery: Density-Guided Query Module for Enhanced 2D and 3D Detection in Crowded Scenes" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">AP</abbr></a> by <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1.8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.8</annotation></semantics></math> and the MR<sup id="S4.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_sup">-2</sup> by <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6.3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6.3</annotation></semantics></math> points.
Considering the MR<sup id="S4.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_sup">-2</sup> metric, our model ranks second, with a <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2.5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.5</annotation></semantics></math> point difference compared to CrowdDet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>, which achieves <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="30.7\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>30.7</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">30.7\%</annotation></semantics></math>.
In general, we observed a less stable MR<sup id="S4.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_sup">-2</sup> performance during training and worse final performance for all query-based approaches compared to the box-based approach.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Next, we discuss the experiments based on the STCrowd 3D detection task, which are presented in Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="TABLE II ‣ IV-A Experimental Setup ‣ IV EXPERIMENTS ‣ CrowdQuery: Density-Guided Query Module for Enhanced 2D and 3D Detection in Crowded Scenes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II</span></a>.
The methods taken from STCrowd <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> (see <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\dagger}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>†</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\dagger}</annotation></semantics></math>) stand out due to their poor performance.
Investigating the difference, we found that the evaluation setting in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> includes all objects in front of the camera within a predefined rectangular area.
Therefore, objects outside the  <a href="#id8"><span href="#id8" title="CrowdQuery: Density-Guided Query Module for Enhanced 2D and 3D Detection in Crowded Scenes" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">FoV</span></a> (<a href="#id8"><abbr href="#id8" title="CrowdQuery: Density-Guided Query Module for Enhanced 2D and 3D Detection in Crowded Scenes" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">FoV</abbr></a>) of the camera are considered during evaluation, which cannot be detected by a camera-only approach.
Filtering based on the camera <a href="#id8"><abbr href="#id8" title="CrowdQuery: Density-Guided Query Module for Enhanced 2D and 3D Detection in Crowded Scenes" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">FoV</abbr></a> reduces the number of ground truth instances by roughly 30%.
When evaluating MonoFlex without and with the additional instances, the <a href="#id5"><abbr href="#id5" title="CrowdQuery: Density-Guided Query Module for Enhanced 2D and 3D Detection in Crowded Scenes" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">mAP</abbr></a> drops from 49.5% to 30.6%.
This explains the large delta between the reported numbers in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> and our experiments.
Still, these numbers indicate a relative improvement of <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3.3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.3</annotation></semantics></math> <a href="#id5"><abbr href="#id5" title="CrowdQuery: Density-Guided Query Module for Enhanced 2D and 3D Detection in Crowded Scenes" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">mAP</abbr></a> points when replacing the ResNet-101 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite> backbone with the more advanced DLA-34 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>.
Our <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CQ3D</span> architecture outperforms the baseline MonoDETR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> for all reported metrics.
The <a href="#id5"><abbr href="#id5" title="CrowdQuery: Density-Guided Query Module for Enhanced 2D and 3D Detection in Crowded Scenes" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">mAP</abbr></a> improves by <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4.2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4.2</annotation></semantics></math>, the <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="AR_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">AR_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3.2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.2</annotation></semantics></math>, the <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="AR_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">AR_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3.4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.4</annotation></semantics></math>, and the <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="AR_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">AR_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3.6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.6</annotation></semantics></math>.
Therefore, our method improves the detection performance using only 2D boxes, which are based on the projected 3D bounding boxes, as a ground truth for the density task.
Furthermore, our method surpasses the <a href="#id5"><abbr href="#id5" title="CrowdQuery: Density-Guided Query Module for Enhanced 2D and 3D Detection in Crowded Scenes" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">mAP</abbr></a> of any other tested method by at least <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3.2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.2</annotation></semantics></math> points, even though it uses the simpler ResNet-50 backbone.
Reviewing the AR, we exhibit lower scores than MonoFlex on fully visible <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(AR_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(AR_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> and lightly occluded <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(AR_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(AR_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> instances, though we perform best on highly occluded instances <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(AR_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(AR_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In addition to the quantitative findings, we also provide qualitative results of both CQ2D and CQ3D in Figure <a href="#S4.F5" title="Fig. 5 ‣ IV-C Ablations ‣ IV EXPERIMENTS ‣ CrowdQuery: Density-Guided Query Module for Enhanced 2D and 3D Detection in Crowded Scenes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">IV-C</span> </span><span id="S4.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Ablations</span></h3>

<figure id="S4.T5" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S4.T5.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">TABLE V</span>: </span><span id="S4.T5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Comparison of architecture configurations tested on STCrowd 2D detection. The final row represents our final and best configuration.</span></figcaption>
<table id="S4.T5.5" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S4.T5.5.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T5.5.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T5.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Embedding</span></td>
<td id="S4.T5.5.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><math id="S4.T5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒅</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{d}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
