</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS4.6" class="ltx_text">IV-D</span> </span><span id="S4.SS4.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Integration into Crowd-Focused Detector</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Besides the integration into general detectors, we investigate the application in crowd-focused detectors.
Due to the lack of suitable 3D detectors, we prioritize 2D detectors and select DDQ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>.
As shown in Table <a href="#S4.T6" title="TABLE VI ‣ IV-C Ablations ‣ IV EXPERIMENTS ‣ CrowdQuery: Density-Guided Query Module for Enhanced 2D and 3D Detection in Crowded Scenes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">VI</span></a>, DDQ already performs well on STCrowd 2D without any modifications.
By adding our <a href="#id7"><abbr href="#id7" title="CrowdQuery: Density-Guided Query Module for Enhanced 2D and 3D Detection in Crowded Scenes" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">CQ</abbr></a> module to DDQ, referred to as CQ2D++, the model outperforms all other methods presented in Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="TABLE I ‣ IV-A Experimental Setup ‣ IV EXPERIMENTS ‣ CrowdQuery: Density-Guided Query Module for Enhanced 2D and 3D Detection in Crowded Scenes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">I</span></a> as well as CQ2D in AP.
Additionally, CQ2D++ closes the gap of the MR<sup id="S4.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_sup">-2</sup> to 0.7 points compared to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>.
Optimal results were achieved by tuning the density loss more aggressively, that is, we increased <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, to account for the model’s pre-existing crowd focus.
In the end, CQ2D++ improves the AP by 2.3 and the MR<sup id="S4.SS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_sup">-2</sup> by 7.1 points.
All experiments are performed using the original DDQ configuration.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS5.6" class="ltx_text">IV-E</span> </span><span id="S4.SS5.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Dataset Transfer</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A major challenge in our study is the lack of suitable 3D datasets.
While there are multiple monocular 3D datasets such as KITTI <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>, they rarely exhibit a significant number of pedestrians, especially not in crowded scenarios.
However, in 2D detection, there are multiple viable datasets.
We choose the widely used CrowdHuman <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> dataset due to its high object density.
To ensure competitiveness, we select our best model, CQ2D++ (cf. Section <a href="#S4.SS4" title="IV-D Integration into Crowd-Focused Detector ‣ IV EXPERIMENTS ‣ CrowdQuery: Density-Guided Query Module for Enhanced 2D and 3D Detection in Crowded Scenes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV-D</span></a>), and evaluate it on the challenging CrowdHuman dataset (see results in Table <a href="#S4.T7" title="TABLE VII ‣ IV-C Ablations ‣ IV EXPERIMENTS ‣ CrowdQuery: Density-Guided Query Module for Enhanced 2D and 3D Detection in Crowded Scenes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">VII</span></a>).
CQ2D++ mainly improves MR<sup id="S4.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_sup">-2</sup> and outperforms all other methods.
Even though results on CrowdHuman have shown to converge over the last publications, we are still able to improve nearly one point in MR<sup id="S4.SS5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_sup">-2</sup> using our easy-to-integrate method.
We kept the optimal settings of CQ2D++ from the previous experiments to highlight easy transferability.
Additional fine-tuning might enhance results further.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS6.6" class="ltx_text">IV-F</span> </span><span id="S4.SS6.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Computation Efficiency</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Table <a href="#S4.T8" title="TABLE VIII ‣ IV-C Ablations ‣ IV EXPERIMENTS ‣ CrowdQuery: Density-Guided Query Module for Enhanced 2D and 3D Detection in Crowded Scenes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">VIII</span></a>, CQ3D introduces a <math id="S4.SS6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="16.0\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>16.0</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16.0\%</annotation></semantics></math> runtime increase, with two-thirds attributed to the self-attention in the <a href="#id7"><abbr href="#id7" title="CrowdQuery: Density-Guided Query Module for Enhanced 2D and 3D Detection in Crowded Scenes" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">CQ</abbr></a> module.
The parameter count increases by just <math id="S4.SS6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12.9\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>12.9</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12.9\%</annotation></semantics></math>, highlighting our method’s computational efficiency.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">V </span><span id="S5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CONCLUSION</span></h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We presented a general method called <a href="#id7"><span href="#id7" title="CrowdQuery: Density-Guided Query Module for Enhanced 2D and 3D Detection in Crowded Scenes" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">CQ</span></a>, which uses object density information to guide queries and enhance both general and crowd detectors.
The method integrates easily into preexisting networks, as long as a query-based decoder is present.
Integrating <a href="#id7"><abbr href="#id7" title="CrowdQuery: Density-Guided Query Module for Enhanced 2D and 3D Detection in Crowded Scenes" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">CQ</abbr></a> into selected base models results in the architectures CQ2D and CQ3D.
Both architectures demonstrate superior performance compared to non-extended versions and mostly outperform or match state-of-the-art methods. This improvement is not limited to the selected base models, as shown by the clear improvement when integrated into the crowd-based model DDQ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>.
Extensive ablation studies validate the effectiveness of our method. To achieve optimal results, the parameters should be adapted to the selected base model.
We hope to inspire others to treat both 2D and 3D crowd detection as a more unified topic in the future.
Future work will explore our method’s generalizability by including more networks and datasets, and by extending it to LiDAR-based detectors.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">ACKNOWLEDGMENT</h2>

